<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Morro Castle (Havana)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1589">1589</a>–1630</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Castillo de los Tres Reyes Magos del Morro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Morro Castle (Castillo de los Tres Reyes Magos del Morro) is the fortress guarding the entrance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> harbour, built by the Spanish from 1589 to about 1630. In 1762 a British army besieged it for weeks; its commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-vicente-de-velasco">Luis Vicente de Velasco</a> was mortally wounded when it was stormed on 30 July, and Havana surrendered soon after. It later served as a prison, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-augustus-bowles">William Augustus Bowles</a> died in 1805.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/morro-castle-havana.jpg" alt="Morro Castle (Havana)" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Photo Company Collection · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuba_-_Morro_Castle,_near_view_LCCN2001706155_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
